course: dictionary
lesson: 12405
title: Formal words for customs
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Customarily is a formal word. It means usually, or the way people normally do things because of tradition or habit. «In Japan people customarily bow when they greet each other.» The sentence tells you that bowing is a tradition there, so customarily means as people usually do.
explain: Look for clues around the word. Words like traditionally, each year or at weddings often sit close to it and point the same way. «At weddings, the couple customarily cut a large cake.» The wedding is the clue: it tells you this is a common custom.
explain: In everyday talk, you can swap customarily for usually or normally. Try your meaning in the sentence: «The class customarily lines up by the door» becomes «The class usually lines up by the door», and it still makes sense. That is a good check.
fact: Customarily means usually, or as people normally do because of custom.
fact: Customarily is a formal word, so you will mostly meet it in books, exams, and history or RE lessons.
fact: A custom is something people do as a tradition or habit.
fact: Everyday words that mean the same are usually and normally.
fact: Customarily is an adverb, so it tells you how something is done, as in «gifts are customarily given at birthdays».
exercise: «Gifts are customarily given at birthdays.» What does customarily mean here? | Secretly, when nobody is looking | *Usually, as people normally do | Rarely, only once in a lifetime | Quickly, without any thought
exercise: Which word could replace customarily in «The class customarily lines up by the door» without changing the meaning? | *Usually | Quietly | Angrily | Rarely
exercise: Mia says customarily is just a fancy way of saying «sometimes». Is she right? | Yes, it means never | *No, it means usually or normally, not just now and then | No, it means very fast | Yes, it means only now and then
test: «In Japan people customarily bow.» Which phrase means the same as customarily here? | *As people usually do | Once a year only | Very carefully, with great effort | In a loud voice
test: Why would a history writer choose customarily instead of usually? | It is only used by children | It is shorter and easier to spell | *It sounds more formal and fits a text about traditions | It means happy
exam: Which sentence uses customarily correctly? | He customarily jumped into the sea in his shoes on purpose. | The cat customarily flew over the roof last night. | *The school customarily holds its sports day in June. | The sky customarily sang a loud song at noon.
